  "status": "published",
  "title": "Earlier puzzle numbers as LP2 rune keys",
  "question": "Do the 2012 P.S., 2013 telnet missing primes, and message-space sequences decode the unsolved rune text under source B3 rules?",
  "reader_question": "Could unused numbers from earlier Cicada puzzles be LP2 keys?",
  "summary": "Replays the published B3 search of nine historical numeric streams against 55 unsolved rune segments and their joined text, with all 4,032 cases independently scored.",
  "finding": "None of 4,032 exact-source decodes crossed the English-score bar of −5.2. The best scored −6.757. Two planted messages were recovered, including an F-interrupted case.",
  "limitations": "The original source reports maxima, not a selection-adjusted null or proof that every true English plaintext would pass its score bar. This does not test onion-cookie hashes, raw PGP packets, other offsets, nonrepeating uses, or different languages. The telnet transcript is a later preserved copy.",
  "next_steps": "For a different artifact-key proposal, pin its original bytes, reduction to rune indices, target, offset and boundary rule before a search.",
  "outcome": "not-supported",
